\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {14}{\ignorespaces The new methods. Each row is one method. \emph  {Decides with}: the distance whose static score is compared with its conformal threshold (level $\beta =0.1$); ``joint'' is the score of Proposition~\ref {pr:combined}(i) for the source slice and the $L^2$ field distance. \emph  {Target}: the distance in which the target is the approved prompt nearest to the request, among the anchors and calibration prompts whose own score is at most the stop level; ``own'' is the deciding distance, as in Section~\ref {sec:e3_enforce}. \emph  {Stops at}: the stop level of the bisection on $\kappa $, either the threshold (the minimal back-off of Proposition~\ref {pr:backoff}) or the median level (the $k'$-th smallest of the $40$ calibration scores, $k'=21$); ``local'': the threshold, and a field distance to the target at most the median nearest-anchor field distance of the calibration prompts. \emph  {Meant to repair}: the failure of a previous method that the new one addresses.}}{45}{table.caption.95}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:e3c_methods}{{14}{45}{The new methods. Each row is one method. \emph {Decides with}: the distance whose static score is compared with its conformal threshold (level $\beta =\ethBeta $); ``joint'' is the score of Proposition~\ref {pr:combined}(i) for the source slice and the $L^2$ field distance. \emph {Target}: the distance in which the target is the approved prompt nearest to the request, among the anchors and calibration prompts whose own score is at most the stop level; ``own'' is the deciding distance, as in Section~\ref {sec:e3_enforce}. \emph {Stops at}: the stop level of the bisection on $\kappa $, either the threshold (the minimal back-off of Proposition~\ref {pr:backoff}) or the median level (the $k'$-th smallest of the $\ethK $ calibration scores, $k'=\ethcKdeep $); ``local'': the threshold, and a field distance to the target at most the median nearest-anchor field distance of the calibration prompts. \emph {Meant to repair}: the failure of a previous method that the new one addresses}{table.caption.95}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{Detection: a joint region adds the false alarms of the field distance, not its detection.}{45}{section*.97}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {15}{\ignorespaces Detection by the joint region of the source slice and the $L^2$ field distance, and by the Bonferroni region of the same two scores (Proposition~\ref {pr:combined}(i) and (ii)), with the two regions that they combine (static weights; evaluation pool, $M=30$, $K=40$, means over $200$ splits). Columns as in Table~\ref {tab:e3_detection}: coverage, then the fraction of each kind of request excluded, higher is better for the three unsafe kinds and lower is better for the two safe unfamiliar kinds; the disguised requests are a mixture of both.}}{45}{table.caption.98}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:e3c_detection}{{15}{45}{Detection by the joint region of the source slice and the $L^2$ field distance, and by the Bonferroni region of the same two scores (Proposition~\ref {pr:combined}(i) and (ii)), with the two regions that they combine (static weights; evaluation pool, $M=\ethM $, $K=\ethK $, means over $\ethNeval $ splits). Columns as in Table~\ref {tab:e3_detection}: coverage, then the fraction of each kind of request excluded, higher is better for the three unsafe kinds and lower is better for the two safe unfamiliar kinds; the disguised requests are a mixture of both}{table.caption.98}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{Enforcement: what each method deploys.}{46}{section*.99}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {16}{\ignorespaces Enforcement by the new methods (first evaluation split, $8$ requests of each kind, $8$ evaluation noises each). Each row is one method; the first block repeats the previous regions with static weights (Table~\ref {tab:e3_enforce}), the second lists the new methods of Table~\ref {tab:e3c_methods}. Columns as in Table~\ref {tab:e3_enforce}. For the summer requests and for the requests without season: the fraction of requests altered (backed off, $\kappa >0$), their mean mixing weight $\kappa $ (from $0$, request unchanged, to $1$, request replaced by its target), and the fraction of their deployed images judged unsafe (lower is better). Then: the fraction of the disguised requests' images judged unsafe; the mean scene similarity of the deployed images of the summer, season-not-mentioned and disguised requests (higher is better; about $0.3$ for the requests' own images, $0.19$ for images of another scene); the fraction of altered requests whose target shows the requested scene; and the fraction of the $6$ approved test prompts that the method alters. $^*$: summer requests only, the only ones given to the projection.}}{46}{table.caption.100}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:e3c_enforce}{{16}{46}{Enforcement by the new methods (first evaluation split, $\ethNreqKind $ requests of each kind, $\ethP $ evaluation noises each). Each row is one method; the first block repeats the previous regions with static weights (Table~\ref {tab:e3_enforce}), the second lists the new methods of Table~\ref {tab:e3c_methods}. Columns as in Table~\ref {tab:e3_enforce}. For the summer requests and for the requests without season: the fraction of requests altered (backed off, $\kappa >0$), their mean mixing weight $\kappa $ (from $0$, request unchanged, to $1$, request replaced by its target), and the fraction of their deployed images judged unsafe (lower is better). Then: the fraction of the disguised requests' images judged unsafe; the mean scene similarity of the deployed images of the summer, season-not-mentioned and disguised requests (higher is better; about $0.3$ for the requests' own images, $\ethSubjOtherMH $ for images of another scene); the fraction of altered requests whose target shows the requested scene; and the fraction of the $\ethNreqApp $ approved test prompts that the method alters. $^*$: summer requests only, the only ones given to the projection}{table.caption.100}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {24}{\ignorespaces What each method deploys, method by method (the numbers are those of Table~\ref {tab:e3c_enforce}). Each row is one method: first ``no region'', every request served as asked; then the previous regions with static weights; then the new methods. In each row, the circle is the mean over the $8$ summer requests and the triangle the mean over the $8$ requests that do not mention the season; the projection was run on the summer requests only. \emph  {Colour}: the distance that decides (aqua: source slice; blue: field $L^2$; violet: Mahalanobis; dark grey: joint score; black: no region). (a) The fraction of deployed images that the judge finds unsafe; lower is better, and the ideal is $0$. (b) Their scene similarity, the judge's cosine between each image and ``a photograph of \{scene\}'' for the requested scene; higher is better. The grey band spans the requests' own images (the most that a repair can keep), and the dotted line marks images of another scene. A good method has both markers near $0$ in (a) and inside or near the grey band in (b).}}{47}{figure.caption.101}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:e3c_enforce_map}{{24}{47}{What each method deploys, method by method (the numbers are those of Table~\ref {tab:e3c_enforce}). Each row is one method: first ``no region'', every request served as asked; then the previous regions with static weights; then the new methods. In each row, the circle is the mean over the $\ethNreqKind $ summer requests and the triangle the mean over the $\ethNreqKind $ requests that do not mention the season; the projection was run on the summer requests only. \emph {Colour}: the distance that decides (aqua: source slice; blue: field $L^2$; violet: Mahalanobis; dark grey: joint score; black: no region). (a) The fraction of deployed images that the judge finds unsafe; lower is better, and the ideal is $0$. (b) Their scene similarity, the judge's cosine between each image and ``a photograph of \{scene\}'' for the requested scene; higher is better. The grey band spans the requests' own images (the most that a repair can keep), and the dotted line marks images of another scene. A good method has both markers near $0$ in (a) and inside or near the grey band in (b)}{figure.caption.101}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {25}{\ignorespaces How deep the back-off goes, request by request. Each pair of points joined by a grey line is one summer (circle) or season-not-mentioned (triangle) request that the deciding region excludes: the hollow point is the back-off that stops at the threshold, the filled point the deeper one; the decision and the target are the same in both. Horizontal axis: the mixing weight $\kappa $ of the target. Vertical axis: the mean probability of winter that the judge gives to the request's $8$ deployed images; below the dashed red line, at $0.5$, the images are mostly unsafe. (a) The source slice decides and the target is chosen in the field distance; the deeper stop is the median level of the source slice. (b) The same, with local depth. (c) The field region, with its median level. A good deeper stop moves every point above the red line with as small an increase in $\kappa $ as possible.}}{48}{figure.caption.102}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:e3c_depth}{{25}{48}{How deep the back-off goes, request by request. Each pair of points joined by a grey line is one summer (circle) or season-not-mentioned (triangle) request that the deciding region excludes: the hollow point is the back-off that stops at the threshold, the filled point the deeper one; the decision and the target are the same in both. Horizontal axis: the mixing weight $\kappa $ of the target. Vertical axis: the mean probability of winter that the judge gives to the request's $\ethP $ deployed images; below the dashed red line, at $0.5$, the images are mostly unsafe. (a) The source slice decides and the target is chosen in the field distance; the deeper stop is the median level of the source slice. (b) The same, with local depth. (c) The field region, with its median level. A good deeper stop moves every point above the red line with as small an increase in $\kappa $ as possible}{figure.caption.102}{}}
