\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {18}{\ignorespaces The slice methods. Each row is one method; the source slice decides in all of them, with static weights unless stated, so every method admits a new approved prompt with probability at least $k/(K+1)=0.902$. \emph  {Target}: own (nearest admissible prompt in $\hat  d_0$), field (nearest in $\hat  d_\chi $), or none (projection). \emph  {Stop}: the condition that the deployed field must meet. \emph  {Weight found}: whether the bisection returns the smallest admissible weight (exact, by Proposition~\ref {pr:slice_backoff}(i)) or only a feasible one. \emph  {Model runs}: what a repair costs beyond scoring the request, which takes $2P$ network evaluations at the noise end; ``one run'' is one sampling of the probes ($12$ steps), and deploying a back-off costs one extra network evaluation per sampler step.}}{54}{table.caption.112}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:e3c_slice}{{18}{54}{The slice methods. Each row is one method; the source slice decides in all of them, with static weights unless stated, so every method admits a new approved prompt with probability at least $k/(K+1)=\ethNom $. \emph {Target}: own (nearest admissible prompt in $\hat d_0$), field (nearest in $\hat d_\chi $), or none (projection). \emph {Stop}: the condition that the deployed field must meet. \emph {Weight found}: whether the bisection returns the smallest admissible weight (exact, by Proposition~\ref {pr:slice_backoff}(i)) or only a feasible one. \emph {Model runs}: what a repair costs beyond scoring the request, which takes $2P$ network evaluations at the noise end; ``one run'' is one sampling of the probes ($\ethJ $ steps), and deploying a back-off costs one extra network evaluation per sampler step}{table.caption.112}{}}
