\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {20}{\ignorespaces The safe and the unsafe set of each space ($M=30$ anchors and $K=40$ calibration prompts of each class; means over $200$ evaluation splits). Each row is one space with static or prompt-adaptive weights; \emph  {weights} names the adaptive weightings chosen on the tuning pools for the safe and for the unsafe set (X:$c$: Gaussian kernel in the prompt distance, bandwidth $c$ times the median prompt distance between anchors; XN:$k$: the $k$ nearest prompts). \emph  {Safe set, coverage}: fraction of the approved test prompts in the safe set (nominal $0.902$, higher is better); \emph  {unsafe in it}: fraction of the unsafe test prompts in the safe set, its size (lower is better). \emph  {Unsafe set, coverage}: fraction of the unsafe test prompts in the unsafe set (nominal $0.902$); \emph  {approved in it}: fraction of the approved test prompts in the unsafe set, its size (lower is better), the $\pi $ of Proposition~\ref {pr:two_sets}(iii). \emph  {Two sets admit}: fraction of the approved test prompts that the two-set rule admits (higher is better). \emph  {Cap binds}: fraction of the splits in which the cap of the rule ``two sets, capped'' lowers the unsafe threshold.}}{58}{table.caption.121}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:e3u_sets}{{20}{58}{The safe and the unsafe set of each space ($M=\ethM $ anchors and $K=\ethK $ calibration prompts of each class; means over $\ethNeval $ evaluation splits). Each row is one space with static or prompt-adaptive weights; \emph {weights} names the adaptive weightings chosen on the tuning pools for the safe and for the unsafe set (X:$c$: Gaussian kernel in the prompt distance, bandwidth $c$ times the median prompt distance between anchors; XN:$k$: the $k$ nearest prompts). \emph {Safe set, coverage}: fraction of the approved test prompts in the safe set (nominal $\ethNom $, higher is better); \emph {unsafe in it}: fraction of the unsafe test prompts in the safe set, its size (lower is better). \emph {Unsafe set, coverage}: fraction of the unsafe test prompts in the unsafe set (nominal $\ethNom $); \emph {approved in it}: fraction of the approved test prompts in the unsafe set, its size (lower is better), the $\pi $ of Proposition~\ref {pr:two_sets}(iii). \emph {Two sets admit}: fraction of the approved test prompts that the two-set rule admits (higher is better). \emph {Cap binds}: fraction of the splits in which the cap of the rule ``two sets, capped'' lowers the unsafe threshold}{table.caption.121}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {28}{\ignorespaces The sets against the number $M$ of anchors of each class (the $190$ prompts of each class pooled; $M$ anchors, $40$ calibration prompts and the rest as test prompts of each class; means over $200$ splits for each $M$). Colour: the space (orange: prompt; aqua: source slice; blue: field). Dashed lines with hollow markers: static weights; solid lines with filled markers: all the weight on the anchor of each class whose prompt is nearest to the request (XN:1). (a) Fraction of the unsafe test prompts that lie in the safe set, and (b) fraction of the approved test prompts that lie in the unsafe set: the size of each set where the other class lies, lower is better. (c) Fraction of the requests that do not mention the season that the two-set rule excludes (unsafe, higher is better), and (d) fraction of the winter requests of new scenes that it excludes (safe, lower is better: false alarms). What to notice: the source slice is near $0$ in (a) and (b) at every $M$; the field and prompt sets shrink with $M$ only with nearest-prompt weights, which also raise the false alarms in (d).}}{59}{figure.caption.122}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:e3u_sizes}{{28}{59}{The sets against the number $M$ of anchors of each class (the $\ethNapp $ prompts of each class pooled; $M$ anchors, $\ethK $ calibration prompts and the rest as test prompts of each class; means over $\ethNeval $ splits for each $M$). Colour: the space (orange: prompt; aqua: source slice; blue: field). Dashed lines with hollow markers: static weights; solid lines with filled markers: all the weight on the anchor of each class whose prompt is nearest to the request (XN:1). (a) Fraction of the unsafe test prompts that lie in the safe set, and (b) fraction of the approved test prompts that lie in the unsafe set: the size of each set where the other class lies, lower is better. (c) Fraction of the requests that do not mention the season that the two-set rule excludes (unsafe, higher is better), and (d) fraction of the winter requests of new scenes that it excludes (safe, lower is better: false alarms). What to notice: the source slice is near $0$ in (a) and (b) at every $M$; the field and prompt sets shrink with $M$ only with nearest-prompt weights, which also raise the false alarms in (d)}{figure.caption.122}{}}
