\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {21}{\ignorespaces Detection by the safe set alone and by the two sets (means over $200$ splits). Each row is one space and weighting; each cell reads ``safe set alone $\to $ two sets''. First block: the protocol of Section~\ref {sec:e3} ($M=30$ anchors of each class, weights tuned on the tuning pools, named in parentheses for the safe and the unsafe set); second block: $M=140$ anchors of each class and the weights of the repair experiment. \emph  {Approved admitted}: fraction of the approved test prompts admitted (higher is better). Then the fraction of each kind of request excluded: the three unsafe kinds (higher is better), the two safe unfamiliar kinds (lower is better: false alarms), and the disguised requests, whose own images are judged unsafe in $0.35$ of the cases.}}{60}{table.caption.123}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:e3u_detection}{{21}{60}{Detection by the safe set alone and by the two sets (means over $\ethNeval $ splits). Each row is one space and weighting; each cell reads ``safe set alone $\to $ two sets''. First block: the protocol of Section~\ref {sec:e3} ($M=\ethM $ anchors of each class, weights tuned on the tuning pools, named in parentheses for the safe and the unsafe set); second block: $M=140$ anchors of each class and the weights of the repair experiment. \emph {Approved admitted}: fraction of the approved test prompts admitted (higher is better). Then the fraction of each kind of request excluded: the three unsafe kinds (higher is better), the two safe unfamiliar kinds (lower is better: false alarms), and the disguised requests, whose own images are judged unsafe in $\ethOwnUnsafeDis $ of the cases}{table.caption.123}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {29}{\ignorespaces What the unsafe set changes in detection ($M=30$ anchors of each class, means over $200$ splits; the numbers are those of the first block of Table~\ref {tab:e3u_detection}). Each arrow is one space with one weighting: it starts at the safe set alone (circle for the prompt and the source slice, square for the field, as in Figure~\ref {fig:e3_detection}) and ends at the two sets (diamond). Colour: the space; hollow markers and dashed arrows: static weights; filled markers and solid arrows: prompt-adaptive weights. Horizontal axis: the fraction of the safe unfamiliar requests (paraphrases and winter scenes that no approved prompt shows) that the rule excludes, its false alarms. Vertical axis: the fraction of the unsafe requests excluded, (a) summer requests, (b) requests that do not mention the season. The ideal rule sits in the upper left corner; an arrow that points straight up adds detection at no cost. What to notice: the source-slice arrows point up, the prompt and field arrows point up and to the right.}}{60}{figure.caption.124}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:e3u_detection}{{29}{60}{What the unsafe set changes in detection ($M=\ethM $ anchors of each class, means over $\ethNeval $ splits; the numbers are those of the first block of Table~\ref {tab:e3u_detection}). Each arrow is one space with one weighting: it starts at the safe set alone (circle for the prompt and the source slice, square for the field, as in Figure~\ref {fig:e3_detection}) and ends at the two sets (diamond). Colour: the space; hollow markers and dashed arrows: static weights; filled markers and solid arrows: prompt-adaptive weights. Horizontal axis: the fraction of the safe unfamiliar requests (paraphrases and winter scenes that no approved prompt shows) that the rule excludes, its false alarms. Vertical axis: the fraction of the unsafe requests excluded, (a) summer requests, (b) requests that do not mention the season. The ideal rule sits in the upper left corner; an arrow that points straight up adds detection at no cost. What to notice: the source-slice arrows point up, the prompt and field arrows point up and to the right}{figure.caption.124}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {30}{\ignorespaces The four cells of each kind of request (means over $200$ splits). Each bar is one kind of request, each panel one space; the bar is divided into the fractions of the requests whose field lies in the safe set only (blue: admitted by the two-set rule), in both sets (violet: ambiguous), in the unsafe set only (red), and in neither (grey: novel). The first two bars are the test prompts of the two histories, whose cells calibration controls (about $0.902$ of each in its own set); the others are the requests. Top row: static weights and $M=30$ anchors of each class; bottom row: the sets of the repair experiment ($M=140$; static weights for the source slice, the nearest prompt of each class for the prompt and the field). The rule of Section~\ref {sec:e3} admits blue and violet; the two-set rule admits blue only; a blacklist would admit blue and grey. What to notice: in the source slice violet is rare, so the two rules differ mainly on the requests that do not mention the season; in the prompt and field rows of the top panel violet covers half of the approved prompts.}}{61}{figure.caption.125}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:e3u_cells}{{30}{61}{The four cells of each kind of request (means over $\ethNeval $ splits). Each bar is one kind of request, each panel one space; the bar is divided into the fractions of the requests whose field lies in the safe set only (blue: admitted by the two-set rule), in both sets (violet: ambiguous), in the unsafe set only (red), and in neither (grey: novel). The first two bars are the test prompts of the two histories, whose cells calibration controls (about $\ethNom $ of each in its own set); the others are the requests. Top row: static weights and $M=\ethM $ anchors of each class; bottom row: the sets of the repair experiment ($M=140$; static weights for the source slice, the nearest prompt of each class for the prompt and the field). The rule of Section~\ref {sec:e3} admits blue and violet; the two-set rule admits blue only; a blacklist would admit blue and grey. What to notice: in the source slice violet is rare, so the two rules differ mainly on the requests that do not mention the season; in the prompt and field rows of the top panel violet covers half of the approved prompts}{figure.caption.125}{}}
