\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {22}{\ignorespaces Repairs ($M=140$ anchors of each class, one split; $8$ requests of each kind, $8$ evaluation noises each). Each row is one method of Table~\ref {tab:e3u_methods}; ``no region'' serves every request as asked; the first block holds the two methods of Section~\ref {sec:e3_combined} rerun on this split, the second the methods with an unsafe set and their control (field, safe set). For the summer requests and for the requests without season: the fraction of requests that the method alters (does not admit unchanged), their mean mixing weight $\kappa $ (from $0$, request unchanged, to $1$, request replaced by its target), and the fraction of their deployed images judged unsafe (lower is better). Then: the fraction of the disguised requests' deployed images judged unsafe; the mean scene similarity of the deployed images of the summer, season-not-mentioned and disguised requests (higher is better; $0.317$ and $0.325$ for the summer and season-not-mentioned requests' own images, about $0.19$ for images of another scene); the fraction of altered requests whose target shows the requested scene; and the fraction of the $5$ approved test prompts that the method alters (lower is better).}}{62}{table.caption.126}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:e3u_enforce}{{22}{62}{Repairs ($M=140$ anchors of each class, one split; $\ethNreqKind $ requests of each kind, $\ethP $ evaluation noises each). Each row is one method of Table~\ref {tab:e3u_methods}; ``no region'' serves every request as asked; the first block holds the two methods of Section~\ref {sec:e3_combined} rerun on this split, the second the methods with an unsafe set and their control (field, safe set). For the summer requests and for the requests without season: the fraction of requests that the method alters (does not admit unchanged), their mean mixing weight $\kappa $ (from $0$, request unchanged, to $1$, request replaced by its target), and the fraction of their deployed images judged unsafe (lower is better). Then: the fraction of the disguised requests' deployed images judged unsafe; the mean scene similarity of the deployed images of the summer, season-not-mentioned and disguised requests (higher is better; $\ethuOwnSubjSum $ and $\ethuOwnSubjNeu $ for the summer and season-not-mentioned requests' own images, about $\ethSubjOtherMH $ for images of another scene); the fraction of altered requests whose target shows the requested scene; and the fraction of the $\ethuNappReq $ approved test prompts that the method alters (lower is better)}{table.caption.126}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {23}{\ignorespaces Unfamiliar requests under the repairs of Table~\ref {tab:e3u_enforce} ($8$ requests of each kind, $8$ evaluation noises each). Each row is one method. For the paraphrases and the winter requests of new scenes, which are safe: the fraction of requests that the method alters, its false alarms (lower is better); the mean image change, the $\chi $-distance between each deployed image and the request's own image on the same noise ($0$ for an admitted request; $0.37$ between two approved prompts of the same scene, $0.71$ between two scenes; lower is better); and the fraction of deployed images judged unsafe. For the summer requests of new scenes, which are unsafe and have no approved prompt of their scene: the fraction altered (higher is better), the fraction of deployed images judged unsafe (lower is better), and their scene similarity (higher is better; $0.309$ for their own images).}}{62}{table.caption.127}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:e3u_unfamiliar}{{23}{62}{Unfamiliar requests under the repairs of Table~\ref {tab:e3u_enforce} ($\ethuNunf $ requests of each kind, $\ethP $ evaluation noises each). Each row is one method. For the paraphrases and the winter requests of new scenes, which are safe: the fraction of requests that the method alters, its false alarms (lower is better); the mean image change, the $\chi $-distance between each deployed image and the request's own image on the same noise ($0$ for an admitted request; $\ethcChgSame $ between two approved prompts of the same scene, $\ethcChgOther $ between two scenes; lower is better); and the fraction of deployed images judged unsafe. For the summer requests of new scenes, which are unsafe and have no approved prompt of their scene: the fraction altered (higher is better), the fraction of deployed images judged unsafe (lower is better), and their scene similarity (higher is better; $\ethuOwnSubjNovS $ for their own images)}{table.caption.127}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {31}{\ignorespaces What each method deploys (the numbers are those of Table~\ref {tab:e3u_enforce}). Each row is one method: first ``no region'', then the two methods of Section~\ref {sec:e3_combined} rerun on this split, then the methods with an unsafe set and their control. In each row, the circle is the mean over the $8$ summer requests and the triangle the mean over the $8$ requests that do not mention the season. Colour: the space that decides (aqua: source slice; orange: prompt; blue: field; black: no region). (a) The fraction of deployed images that the judge finds unsafe; lower is better. (b) Their scene similarity; higher is better. The grey band spans the requests' own images, the most that a repair can keep, and the dotted line marks images of another scene. A good method has both markers near $0$ in (a) and inside or near the grey band in (b).}}{63}{figure.caption.128}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:e3u_enforce}{{31}{63}{What each method deploys (the numbers are those of Table~\ref {tab:e3u_enforce}). Each row is one method: first ``no region'', then the two methods of Section~\ref {sec:e3_combined} rerun on this split, then the methods with an unsafe set and their control. In each row, the circle is the mean over the $\ethNreqKind $ summer requests and the triangle the mean over the $\ethNreqKind $ requests that do not mention the season. Colour: the space that decides (aqua: source slice; orange: prompt; blue: field; black: no region). (a) The fraction of deployed images that the judge finds unsafe; lower is better. (b) Their scene similarity; higher is better. The grey band spans the requests' own images, the most that a repair can keep, and the dotted line marks images of another scene. A good method has both markers near $0$ in (a) and inside or near the grey band in (b)}{figure.caption.128}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {32}{\ignorespaces Three repairs along their whole segment. Each panel is one request, mixed with the target of slice$\to $field, two sets: the horizontal axis is the mixing weight $\kappa $ (left: the request's own field; right: its target's), the vertical axis a score of the mixture divided by its threshold, with the request's weights. Aqua: the source slice (static weights; exact, on a fine grid of $\kappa $); blue: the field (nearest prompt; each point a sampling of the mixture on the probes). Solid lines: safe sets, inside when below $1$; dashed lines: unsafe sets, inside when below $1$. A mixture is admissible when both solid lines are below $1$ and both dashed lines above it. Markers above the horizontal axis, at three heights so that none hides another: the weight at which three methods stopped (triangle: slice$\to $field, at the entry into the slice's safe set; square: slice, two sets; diamond: slice$\to $field, two sets, whose bisection on the field has a resolution of $1/16$). What to notice: in (a) and (b) the solid aqua line crosses $1$ before the dashed aqua line does, so the slice's unsafe set sets the depth; in (c) the dashed aqua line starts above $1$, and only the field's safe set asks for more.}}{63}{figure.caption.129}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:e3u_segment}{{32}{63}{Three repairs along their whole segment. Each panel is one request, mixed with the target of slice$\to $field, two sets: the horizontal axis is the mixing weight $\kappa $ (left: the request's own field; right: its target's), the vertical axis a score of the mixture divided by its threshold, with the request's weights. Aqua: the source slice (static weights; exact, on a fine grid of $\kappa $); blue: the field (nearest prompt; each point a sampling of the mixture on the probes). Solid lines: safe sets, inside when below $1$; dashed lines: unsafe sets, inside when below $1$. A mixture is admissible when both solid lines are below $1$ and both dashed lines above it. Markers above the horizontal axis, at three heights so that none hides another: the weight at which three methods stopped (triangle: slice$\to $field, at the entry into the slice's safe set; square: slice, two sets; diamond: slice$\to $field, two sets, whose bisection on the field has a resolution of $1/16$). What to notice: in (a) and (b) the solid aqua line crosses $1$ before the dashed aqua line does, so the slice's unsafe set sets the depth; in (c) the dashed aqua line starts above $1$, and only the field's safe set asks for more}{figure.caption.129}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {33}{\ignorespaces How the unsafe set changes the depth of a repair, request by request. Each pair of points joined by a grey line is one summer (circle) or season-not-mentioned (triangle) request repaired by both methods of a panel: the hollow point is the method without the unsafe set, the filled point the method with it. Horizontal axis: the mixing weight $\kappa $ of the target. Vertical axis: the mean probability of winter that the judge gives to the request's $8$ deployed images; below the dashed red line, at $0.5$, the images are mostly unsafe. (a) slice$\to $field against slice, two sets (source slice: the unsafe set added to the decision and to the stop); (b) field, safe set against field, two sets; (c) slice$\to $field, local against slice$\to $field, two sets (the local stop of Section~\ref {sec:e3_combined} against the field's two sets). A good change moves every point above the red line with as small an increase in $\kappa $ as possible.}}{64}{figure.caption.130}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:e3u_depth}{{33}{64}{How the unsafe set changes the depth of a repair, request by request. Each pair of points joined by a grey line is one summer (circle) or season-not-mentioned (triangle) request repaired by both methods of a panel: the hollow point is the method without the unsafe set, the filled point the method with it. Horizontal axis: the mixing weight $\kappa $ of the target. Vertical axis: the mean probability of winter that the judge gives to the request's $\ethP $ deployed images; below the dashed red line, at $0.5$, the images are mostly unsafe. (a) slice$\to $field against slice, two sets (source slice: the unsafe set added to the decision and to the stop); (b) field, safe set against field, two sets; (c) slice$\to $field, local against slice$\to $field, two sets (the local stop of Section~\ref {sec:e3_combined} against the field's two sets). A good change moves every point above the red line with as small an increase in $\kappa $ as possible}{figure.caption.130}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {34}{\ignorespaces What the methods deploy for three requests that do not mention the season (first three rows; those of Figure~\ref {fig:e3c_gallery_neutral}) and one approved test prompt of this split (last row). The text of each request is at the left of its row. The first column is the request's own image; the others are the deployed images of the methods named in the column titles, all generated from the same evaluation noise. The label in the lower left corner gives the judge's probability of winter (red: judged unsafe) and, when the method repairs the request, the mixing weight $\kappa $; a label without $\kappa $ means that the request is admitted unchanged. What to notice: in the first row, slice$\to $field leaves ``a pine forest, at midday'' a summer forest and the prompt's two sets admit it unchanged, while every method that stops outside an unsafe set or at the local level turns it into a snowy pine forest of the same layout; in the third row, the slice's unsafe set does not recognise the farmhouse, so slice, two sets stops where slice$\to $field does and the barn stays in summer, and only the methods whose stop involves the field (or the local level) frost it; the approved prompt of the last row is left unchanged by every method.}}{64}{figure.caption.131}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:e3u_gallery_neutral}{{34}{64}{What the methods deploy for three requests that do not mention the season (first three rows; those of Figure~\ref {fig:e3c_gallery_neutral}) and one approved test prompt of this split (last row). The text of each request is at the left of its row. The first column is the request's own image; the others are the deployed images of the methods named in the column titles, all generated from the same evaluation noise. The label in the lower left corner gives the judge's probability of winter (red: judged unsafe) and, when the method repairs the request, the mixing weight $\kappa $; a label without $\kappa $ means that the request is admitted unchanged. What to notice: in the first row, slice$\to $field leaves ``a pine forest, at midday'' a summer forest and the prompt's two sets admit it unchanged, while every method that stops outside an unsafe set or at the local level turns it into a snowy pine forest of the same layout; in the third row, the slice's unsafe set does not recognise the farmhouse, so slice, two sets stops where slice$\to $field does and the barn stays in summer, and only the methods whose stop involves the field (or the local level) frost it; the approved prompt of the last row is left unchanged by every method}{figure.caption.131}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {35}{\ignorespaces As Figure~\ref {fig:e3u_gallery_neutral}, for two summer requests (first two rows) and two disguised requests (last two rows), those of Figure~\ref {fig:e3c_gallery_summer}. What to notice: every method turns the two summer requests into winter versions of their scene, and slice$\to $field, which stops as soon as the mixture enters the slice's safe set, mixes least and keeps the most green; the disguised lighthouse, whose own images are already wintry, is altered by the prompt's two sets only (its words are those of a summer prompt), and the disguised meadow by every method with an unsafe set and by none without, its summer content being what the unsafe sets recognise.}}{65}{figure.caption.132}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:e3u_gallery_summer}{{35}{65}{As Figure~\ref {fig:e3u_gallery_neutral}, for two summer requests (first two rows) and two disguised requests (last two rows), those of Figure~\ref {fig:e3c_gallery_summer}. What to notice: every method turns the two summer requests into winter versions of their scene, and slice$\to $field, which stops as soon as the mixture enters the slice's safe set, mixes least and keeps the most green; the disguised lighthouse, whose own images are already wintry, is altered by the prompt's two sets only (its words are those of a summer prompt), and the disguised meadow by every method with an unsafe set and by none without, its summer content being what the unsafe sets recognise}{figure.caption.132}{}}
